# Secrets & SDKs

## Where a value lives

| Value | Home | Reaches the app binary? |
|---|---|---|
| Clerk publishable key | `Config/Secrets.xcconfig` | yes (it is publishable) |
| Convex deployment URL | `Config/Secrets.xcconfig` | yes |
| RevenueCat public API key | `Config/Secrets.xcconfig` | yes |
| PostHog project key, Sentry DSN, OneSignal App ID | `Config/Secrets.xcconfig` | yes |
| FeatureBase portal URLs | `Config/Secrets.xcconfig` | yes |
| **Stripe secret key** | Convex env (`npx convex env set`) | **never** |
| **Clerk JWT issuer domain** | Convex env | never |

`Config/Secrets.xcconfig` is gitignored. `Config/Base.xcconfig` holds
`REPLACE_ME` placeholders and is committed. Never put a real key in
`Base.xcconfig`, `Info.plist` or a `.swift` file.

xcconfig gotcha: `//` starts a comment, so URLs are written
`https:$(SLASH)$(SLASH)example.com`.

## Reading configuration

Only `App/AppConfig.swift` touches `Bundle.main`. It returns `nil` for anything
still containing `REPLACE_ME`, so:

```swift
guard let apiKey = AppConfig.postHogAPIKey else {
    Log.analytics.warning("PostHog is not configured — set POSTHOG_API_KEY …")
    return
}
```

Never `!`, never `fatalError`, never a silent no-op with no log line.

## Integrating an SDK

1. Add the package in Xcode. Prefer a vendor's SPM-specific repo when one exists
   (`OneSignal-XCFramework`, `purchases-ios-spm`) — the main repos carry
   multi-gigabyte histories. See `docs/PACKAGES.md`.
2. Create `Services/<Vendor>/`. Import the SDK there only.
3. Wrap the SDK in `#if canImport(TheModule) && FEATURE_X` so removing either
   the package or the flag still compiles.
4. Expose plain Swift types to `Features/` — structs and enums you own, never
   the SDK's.
5. Add the key to `Base.xcconfig` (placeholder), `Secrets.example.xcconfig`
   (documented example), `Info.plist` `STConfig`, and `AppConfig.Key`.
6. Document it in `docs/SETUP.md`.

## Logging

`Log.<category>` from `Core/Extensions/Log.swift`. Never `print`. Never log a
token, key or full JWT — mark anything user-derived `privacy: .public` only when
you are sure it is not sensitive.
